---
// Safe to edit by hand
// scaffold-file: church
// Page-builder block: the brown band, redrawn for the art-direction pass
// (2026-09-21, spec section 5, the prototype's `.building` block).
//
// It was a 7/5 text-and-picture split with a gold rule under the heading, which
// is the layout every other band on the page was already drawing. The shape now
// is the prototype's: a DETAIL STRIP of up to three photographs running edge to
// edge above the band, then the brown field carrying the h2 on the left six
// columns, the paragraph and its text link on the right five, and a row of
// dated numerals under the heading.
//
// Four things are worth knowing before editing it.
//
// 1. THE STRIP IS BORROWED, AND THE BAND'S OWN PICTURE GOES LAST.
//    SectionRenderer hands down `extraImages` (the first two entries of the
//    page's spare-image pool, src/lib/spare-images.ts) and the block's own
//    `image` is appended AFTER them, because the extras are the pictures
//    nothing else on the page is showing and the strip reads best with the
//    familiar one at the end.
//
// 2. THE BAND'S OWN PICTURE IS DROPPED WHEN THE HERO ALREADY SHOWS IT.
//    SectionRenderer also passes `heroFirstRef`, the asset ref of the first
//    frame of the page's first hero. That frame is the LCP image and is the
//    one picture a visitor has certainly seen, so repeating it two screens
//    later at a different crop reads as a mistake rather than as two
//    distances. The drop is skipped when it would leave the strip empty: a
//    band with no strip at all is a worse outcome than one repeat.
//
// 3. THE NUMERAL ROW IS DERIVED, NEVER TYPED (CLAUDE.md rule 15). The years
//    come from the page's first `timelineSection`: its first row, its middle
//    row and its last. Nothing is stored twice, so the board and the timeline
//    cannot disagree. A page with no timeline renders no numeral row.
//
// 5. WITH DATES IT IS A DIFFERENT BAND: "Our Building" (2026-09-23, the Home
//    identity pass, prototype docs/superpowers/prototypes/2026-09-23-home/
//    home.html). The look is chosen by the CONTENT, never by a colour field
//    (CLAUDE.md rule 9): a band whose `dates` survive heritageDates() draws on
//    a FIXED cream ground (--color-bg never flips, so the band is cream in
//    both themes, like the gold give band is gold in both) with the first and
//    last years set large above the heading, the block's own `image` as the
//    main picture sitting straight on the paper (no frame, no shadow: it is
//    multiplied into the cream, so a drawing's white paper becomes the band's
//    paper), the `archive` photograph in a door arch at a third of the row or
//    more, and the dated list ending in the present with a gold rule. The
//    present's year is the build year (src/lib/heritage-dates.ts, rule 15).
//    A band with no dates (/visit's building band) is the brown band
//    described above, unchanged; the strip, the hero-first drop and the
//    timeline numerals belong to that band only.
//
// 7. AS A PAGE'S OPENER IT IS A THIRD BAND (2026-09-24, the History identity
//    pass): a band with no dates that carries the page's h1 (level 'h1', which
//    SectionRenderer passes only to a first block on a page with no hero, so
//    today /history alone) is drawn by HeritageOpener.astro: the church's
//    brown, the founding year and the build year set large over the h1, and
//    the block's `image` in a door arch with `archive` in a lancet beside it.
//
// 6. AN UNDATED h2 BAND IS "OUR BUILDING" ON CREAM (2026-09-24, the Visit
//    identity pass; the prototype's `.building`,
//    docs/superpowers/prototypes/2026-09-23-visit/visit.html). Visit's building
//    band sits between the deep indigo questions and the gold closing band, so
//    it takes the same FIXED cream ground as the dated band: the year the
//    band's own text names ("completed in 1929") set large above the heading
//    (bandYear(), derived, never typed, and skipped when the heading already
//    says it), the heading in the one band grammar, the paragraph as a lede,
//    the gold button, and the photograph beside it as a plain rectangle when
//    it is of the building (photo-subject.ts) or in a door arch when it is of
//    people. The h1 opener (/history) is note 7.
//
// 4. SELF_CONTAINED: brown in both themes (globals.css moves --color-brown only
//    to a darker brown under .dark), so the ink on it is text-bg, which is
//    paper-white in both themes, rather than text-cream, which flips to ink
//    under .dark.
import SectionHeading from '@/components/SectionHeading.astro';
import { headlineScale } from '@/lib/headline-scale';
import SanityImage from '@/components/SanityImage.astro';
import CtaLink from '@/components/CtaLink.astro';
import ArchFrame from '@/components/church/ArchFrame.astro';
import HeritageOpener from '@/components/sections/HeritageOpener.astro';
import {
  bandYear,
  heritageBookends,
  heritageDates,
  type HeritageDateInput,
} from '@/lib/heritage-dates';
import { photoSubject } from '@/lib/photo-subject';
import { splitStega } from '@/lib/preview-stega';
import type { SanityImageObject } from '@/lib/pageBuilder.types';

/** One dated numeral: the timeline row's marker over its title. */
export interface HeritageYear {
  year: string;
  label: string;
}

interface Props {
  eyebrow?: string;
  heading?: string;
  body?: string;
  image?: any;
  /** An old photograph, drawn in a door arch beside the dates (note 5). */
  archive?: SanityImageObject | null;
  /** The dated list as typed. Any surviving entry makes this the cream band (note 5). */
  dates?: ReadonlyArray<HeritageDateInput | null | undefined> | null;
  cta?: any;
  /**
   * Up to two photographs from elsewhere on the page, in page order, chosen by
   * SectionRenderer from the shared spare-image pool. They lead the strip.
   */
  extraImages?: SanityImageObject[];
  /**
   * The asset ref of the first frame of the page's first hero, or null. See
   * note 2: the band's own picture is dropped from the strip when it matches.
   */
  heroFirstRef?: string | null;
  /** Up to three derived numerals. Empty or absent renders no row. See note 3. */
  years?: HeritageYear[];
  /**
   * The heading tag. 'h2' everywhere except when this band OPENS a page, in
   * which case it is the page's only h1 and SectionRenderer passes 'h1'.
   * /history is the one page in the site that opens on this band rather than
   * on a hero, and a page with no h1 fails axe's page-has-heading-one.
   */
  level?: 'h1' | 'h2';
  headingId: string;
}

const {
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  image,
  archive = null,
  dates = null,
  cta,
  extraImages = [],
  heroFirstRef = null,
  years = [],
  level = 'h2',
  headingId,
} = Astro.props as Props;

// NOTE 7: the band opens its page. A plain boolean rather than an inline
// `level === 'h1'` test in the template, so TypeScript does not narrow `level`
// to 'h2' inside the brown band below, whose own h1 handling stays as it was.
const opensPage: boolean = level === 'h1';

// NOTE 5: the dated list, with the present's year filled in from the build.
const shownDates = heritageDates(dates, new Date());
const ledger = shownDates.length > 0;
const building = !ledger && level === 'h2';
const headingClean = splitStega(heading ?? '').cleaned;
const bigYear = (() => {
  const y = bandYear(null, body);
  return y && !headingClean.includes(y) ? y : null;
})();
const imageAlt = splitStega(((image as { alt?: string } | null)?.alt ?? '') as string).cleaned;
const imageIsPeople = photoSubject(imageAlt) === 'people';
const bookends = heritageBookends(shownDates);
const hasArchive = !!archive?.asset;
const archiveAlt = (() => {
  const alt = (archive as { alt?: unknown } | null)?.alt;
  return typeof alt === 'string' ? alt : '';
})();

// THE STRIP. Extras first, the block's own picture last (note 1).
//
// The identity of a picture is its asset ref, never the object: two blocks
// pointing at one photograph are two different objects, and /history proved it
// by putting the tower in cell one and again in cell three.
const refOf = (img: unknown): string | null => {
  const asset = (img as { asset?: { _ref?: string; _id?: string } } | undefined)?.asset;
  return asset?._ref ?? asset?._id ?? null;
};
const seenRefs = new Set<string>();
const extras = (extraImages ?? []).filter((img) => {
  if (!img?.asset) return false;
  const ref = refOf(img);
  if (ref && seenRefs.has(ref)) return false;
  if (ref) seenRefs.add(ref);
  return true;
});
const ownRef: string | null = refOf(image);
// Null-safe on both sides: a page with no hero passes null, and a projection
// that carries no ref at all must never compare equal to one that does.
const ownIsHeroFirst = !!ownRef && !!heroFirstRef && ownRef === heroFirstRef;
// Keep the band's own picture unless the hero already shows it, and keep it
// anyway when dropping it would leave nothing at all (note 2).
// ...and never when one of the extras is already the same photograph.
const ownIsDuplicate = !!ownRef && seenRefs.has(ownRef);
const keepOwn = !!image?.asset && !ownIsDuplicate && (!ownIsHeroFirst || extras.length === 0);
const strip: SanityImageObject[] = keepOwn ? [...extras, image] : extras;

// The strip's column rhythm follows how many pictures there are. One is a
// single full-width band-height photograph; two split 5/3; three take the
// prototype's 5/3/4. On a phone every strip is two columns of 44vw rows with
// the first picture spanning both, which is the prototype's own breakpoint.
const stripCols =
  strip.length >= 3
    ? 'md:grid-cols-[5fr_3fr_4fr]'
    : strip.length === 2
      ? 'md:grid-cols-[5fr_3fr]'
      : 'md:grid-cols-1';

// `md:grid-rows-1` is load-bearing, and the reason is worth writing down. The
// strip's height is on the CONTAINER; the row was left implicit and auto-sized,
// which means it takes the photograph's natural height and the picture then
// spills out of the band and slides under the words. `grid-rows-1` is
// `repeat(1, minmax(0, 1fr))`, so the one row is exactly the container's
// height, `h-full` on the image resolves against something definite, and
// `overflow-hidden` on each cell is the belt to that pair of braces.

// One picture fills the width, so it is served at full width; two or three
// share it and never need more than half.
const stripSizes = strip.length === 1 ? '100vw' : '(min-width: 768px) 50vw, 100vw';
---

{
  building ? (
    <section class="bg-bg text-band-brown" aria-labelledby={heading ? headingId : undefined}>
      <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-content grid-cols-1 items-center gap-y-12 px-gutter py-section-lg lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,6fr)_minmax(0,5fr)] lg:gap-x-[clamp(40px,6vw,104px)]">
        <div>
          {bigYear && (
            <p
              class="mb-[clamp(10px,1.4vw,20px)] font-display text-[clamp(5.25rem,10vw,9.375rem)] leading-[0.86] tracking-[0.01em] text-band-indigo lining-nums"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {bigYear}
            </p>
          )}
          <SectionHeading
            eyebrow={eyebrow}
            headline={heading}
            headingId={headingId}
            level="h2"
            align="left"
            tone="paper"
            face="display"
          />
          {body && (
            <p class="mt-[clamp(20px,2.2vw,30px)] max-w-[30em] font-body text-[clamp(1.25rem,1rem+.6vw,1.4375rem)] leading-[1.55] text-band-brown">
              {body}
            </p>
          )}
          {cta?.label && (
            <div class="mt-[clamp(28px,3vw,40px)]">
              <CtaLink cta={cta} variant="gold" />
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
        {image?.asset &&
          (imageIsPeople ? (
            <ArchFrame
              image={image}
              shape="door"
              width={1000}
              sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 40vw, 100vw"
              alt={imageAlt}
              grade="warm"
              class="w-full max-w-[32rem]"
              style="--arch-mould: var(--color-brown-mid);"
            />
          ) : (
            <figure class="hb-rect m-0 w-full max-w-[32rem] lg:max-w-none" data-reveal>
              <SanityImage
                source={image}
                width={1000}
                sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 40vw, 100vw"
                quality={78}
                alt={imageAlt}
                class="graded h-full w-full object-cover"
                style="object-position: 50% 30%;"
              />
            </figure>
          ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  ) : ledger ? (
    <section class="bg-bg text-band-brown" aria-labelledby={heading ? headingId : undefined}>
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter py-section-lg">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-12 items-start gap-x-[clamp(16px,2.5vw,40px)] gap-y-10">
          <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-5">
            {/* The first and last years, large. Decorative: the same years are
              read out in the list below. */}
            {bookends.length > 0 && (
              <p
                class="mb-[clamp(20px,2.4vw,32px)] flex items-baseline gap-[0.18em] font-display text-[clamp(3.25rem,2.4rem+2.6vw,5rem)] leading-[0.86] tracking-[0.01em] text-band-indigo lining-nums"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <span>{bookends[0]}</span>
                {bookends[1] && (
                  <>
                    <span class="font-body text-[0.42em] tracking-normal text-brown-mid italic">
                      &amp;
                    </span>
                    <span>{bookends[1]}</span>
                  </>
                )}
              </p>
            )}
            <SectionHeading
              eyebrow={eyebrow}
              headline={heading}
              headingId={headingId}
              level={level}
              scale={level === 'h1' ? headlineScale(heading, { tall: false }) : undefined}
              align="left"
              tone="paper"
              face="display"
            />
            {body && (
              <p class="mt-6 max-w-[30em] font-body text-[clamp(1.25rem,1rem+0.8vw,1.6875rem)] leading-[1.4] text-band-indigo italic">
                {body}
              </p>
            )}
          </div>

          {/* The main picture, straight onto the paper: no frame, no shadow.
            The grade, the multiply and the edge fade are .ob-drawing in
            globals.css. */}
          {image?.asset && (
            <figure class="ob-drawing col-span-12 m-0 lg:col-span-7" data-reveal>
              <SanityImage
                source={image}
                width={1400}
                sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 58vw, 100vw"
                quality={80}
                loading="lazy"
                class="h-auto w-full"
              />
            </figure>
          )}
        </div>

        <div class="mt-[clamp(56px,6vw,96px)] grid grid-cols-12 items-start gap-x-[clamp(16px,2.5vw,40px)] gap-y-10">
          {hasArchive && (
            <ArchFrame
              image={archive}
              shape="door"
              width={900}
              sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 38vw, (min-width: 640px) 66vw, 100vw"
              alt={archiveAlt}
              class="col-span-12 sm:col-span-8 lg:col-span-5"
              style="--arch-ratio: 100 / 108; --arch-mould: var(--color-brown-mid);"
            />
          )}
          <ol
            class:list={[
              'col-span-12 m-0 grid list-none grid-cols-[fit-content(7rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-[clamp(16px,2.4vw,36px)] p-0 sm:grid-cols-[fit-content(13rem)_minmax(0,1fr)]',
              hasArchive ? 'lg:col-span-7' : 'lg:col-span-8 lg:col-start-5',
            ]}
          >
            {/* The year column is ONE track shared by every row (each <li> is
                a subgrid), sized to the widest year: a single year keeps the
                minimum on the year itself, a range like "1921 to 1929" widens
                the whole column up to the fit-content cap and then wraps
                inside it, so the sentences still start on one edge. */}
            {shownDates.map((d) => (
              <li
                class:list={[
                  'col-span-2 grid grid-cols-subgrid items-baseline py-[clamp(16px,1.8vw,22px)]',
                  d.now ? 'border-t-[3px] border-gold' : 'border-t border-band-brown/25',
                ]}
                data-reveal
              >
                <span class="min-w-[4.25rem] font-display text-[clamp(1.5rem,1.1rem+1.6vw,2.5rem)] leading-[1.05] text-band-indigo lining-nums sm:min-w-[6.5rem]">
                  {d.year}
                </span>
                <p class="m-0 max-w-[36em] font-body text-[1.09375rem] leading-[1.5]">{d.text}</p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        {cta?.label && (
          <div class="mt-[clamp(40px,4vw,64px)]">
            <CtaLink cta={cta} variant="gold" />
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </section>
  ) : opensPage ? (
    <HeritageOpener
      eyebrow={eyebrow}
      heading={heading}
      body={body}
      image={image}
      archive={archive}
      cta={cta}
      firstMarker={years[0]?.year}
      headingId={headingId}
    />
  ) : (
    <section class="bg-brown text-bg" aria-labelledby={heading ? headingId : undefined}>
      {/* The detail strip, edge to edge and OUTSIDE the container on purpose: it
      is a band of photograph, not a figure inside a column. */}
      {strip.length > 0 && (
        <div
          class:list={[
            'grid h-auto auto-rows-[44vw] grid-cols-2 gap-1.5 md:h-[clamp(320px,52vw,680px)] md:grid-rows-1',
            stripCols,
          ]}
          data-reveal
        >
          {strip.map((img, i) => (
            <div
              class:list={[
                'min-h-0 overflow-hidden',
                // On a phone the grid is two columns of 44vw. The FIRST picture
                // always spans both, which is the prototype's own breakpoint;
                // so does the second when there are only two, because a lone
                // half-width cell leaves an empty half-band of brown beside it.
                (i === 0 || strip.length === 2) && 'col-span-2 md:col-span-1',
              ]}
            >
              <SanityImage
                source={img}
                width={1200}
                sizes={stripSizes}
                quality={72}
                loading="lazy"
                class="h-full w-full object-cover"
              />
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      )}

      <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-content grid-cols-12 gap-x-[clamp(16px,2.5vw,40px)] px-gutter py-section-lg">
        <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-6">
          <SectionHeading
            eyebrow={eyebrow}
            headline={heading}
            headingId={headingId}
            level={level}
            scale={level === 'h1' ? headlineScale(heading, { tall: false }) : undefined}
            align="left"
            tone="band"
            face="display"
          />
        </div>

        <div class="col-span-12 mt-8 self-end lg:col-span-5 lg:col-start-8 lg:mt-0">
          {body && (
            <p class="max-w-[42ch] font-body text-[1.125rem] leading-[1.72] text-bg/80">{body}</p>
          )}

          {cta?.label && (
            <div class="mt-7">
              <CtaLink cta={cta} variant="link" onDark />
            </div>
          )}
        </div>

        {/* The derived numerals (note 3). Old-style figures, because these are
        dates set beside prose rather than a scoreboard. */}
        {years.length > 0 && (
          <div class="col-span-12 mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-10 lg:col-span-6" data-reveal>
            {years.map((y) => (
              <div class="border-l border-bg/18 pl-4">
                <div class="font-body text-4xl font-normal text-bg oldstyle">{y.year}</div>
                <div class="mt-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-bg/70">{y.label}</div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </section>
  )
}

<style is:global>
  /* Moved out of globals.css (2026-09-24, rule 20): Astro bundles a
     component's styles only into the pages that render it. is:global because
     the rules reach into ArchFrame, PortableText and SanityImage children. */
  /* Our building, undated (HeritageBand.astro note 6): a building photo is a
   plain rectangle, 4:5 beside the words, 5:4 when stacked. */
  .hb-rect {
    aspect-ratio: 5 / 4;
  }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .hb-rect {
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    }
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  /* Moved out of globals.css (2026-09-24, rule 20). */
  /* Our Building (HeritageBand.astro note 5): the main picture sits straight on
   the fixed cream band, no frame and no shadow. The grade lifts a drawing's
   paper to white and the multiply turns that white into the band's cream; the
   mask fades every edge into the page, so a scanned plate's own border and
   any tone left in its paper never draw a rectangle. The figure carries the
   same cream and isolates, so the blend still lands on cream while a reveal
   composites the figure on its own layer. */
  .ob-drawing {
    background: var(--color-bg);
    isolation: isolate;
  }
  .ob-drawing img {
    mix-blend-mode: multiply;
    filter: grayscale(1) contrast(1.12) brightness(1.1) sepia(0.14);
    --ob-fade: linear-gradient(to right, transparent 2.5%, #000 11%, #000 89%, transparent 97.5%);
    mask-image:
      var(--ob-fade),
      linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 3.5%, #000 14%, #000 86%, transparent 96.5%);
    mask-composite: intersect;
  }
</style>
